      "print_war": "Print + War",
      "print_without_galileo_s_name_hidden_im": "Print without Galileo's name, hidden imprint",
      "printing_is_the_wonder_of_all_wonders_": "Printing is the wonder of all wonders. It is the gift of God, by which old things may be made new and new things made known.",
      "printing_made_the_pages_but_a_stack_of": "Printing made the pages — but a stack of paper is not a book. Fold (folio, quarto, octavo), gather, sew, bind. Interactive simulator of the bindery work that followed every print run.",
      "printingpress": "📜 PrintingPress",
      "printingpress_an_interactive_printing_": "PrintingPress: an interactive printing-press history and simulation tool. AlloFlow STEAM Lab (Pomeranz, A., ",
      "printingpress_error": "PrintingPress error: ",
      "printingpress_is_part_of_alloflow_crea": "PrintingPress is part of AlloFlow, created by Dr. Aaron Pomeranz (school psychologist) for accessible + universal-design-for-learning-aligned educational tools. The tool serves K-12 + adult learners studying print history, typography, book arts, publishing, + adjacent cultural fields. The tool is open-source within the AlloFlow ecosystem + freely available to educators + students.",
      "project_gutenberg": "Project Gutenberg",
      "proofreader_s_eye": "🔍 Proofreader’s eye",
      "proportional_reasoning_and_functions_p": " Proportional reasoning and functions (pamphlet reach calculator + mechanical-advantage screw math).",
      "public_libraries": "Public libraries",
      "publick_occurrences": "Publick Occurrences",
      "publisher_or_editor_in_chief": "Publisher or editor-in-chief",
      "pull_any_hardback_off_a_shelf_look_at_": "Pull any hardback off a shelf. Look at the title page, the colophon (back of the title page), the type, the leading. You now know what every choice means.",
      "pull_the_bar_watch_the_screw_turn_the_": "Pull the bar, watch the screw turn, the platen drop, the type ink the paper. Mechanical advantage, force, and historical accuracy. The wow tile.",
      "pulling_the_bar_250_times_a_day_every_": "Pulling the bar 250 times a day, every working day, year after year, wore out shoulders and elbows. By age 50 many pressmen had lost the strength for full-day work. The Linotype operator inherited the same kind of injury in a different shape (keyboard) 400 years later.",
      "pulp_cover_art": "Pulp Cover Art",
      "pulp_era": "Pulp era",
